{"nodes": [{"id": "questions_17_fault_tolerance_question", "label": "Q17: Failures and fault tolerance", "file_type": "document", "source_file": "docs/questions/17-fault-tolerance.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "questions_17_fault_tolerance_four_layers", "label": "The four resilience layers", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/questions/17-fault-tolerance.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Timeout, retry with backoff and jitter, circuit breaker, graceful degradation. The design question is not how to stop failures but what happens when this fails; underneath all four, asynchronous messaging is the structural answer, because you cannot make a synchronous call fault-tolerant, only make it fail better."}, {"id": "questions_17_fault_tolerance_bulkhead", "label": "Bulkhead \u2014 one HTTP client per dependency", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/questions/17-fault-tolerance.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "A shared breaker would let a sick KPI service trip the breaker for Risk too, turning partial degradation into total outage."}, {"id": "questions_17_fault_tolerance_cascading_failure", "label": "The thread-pool cascade", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/questions/17-fault-tolerance.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "B slows down, A's threads block, A's thread pool exhausts, A stops answering anything including requests that never needed B. Timeouts and breakers exist specifically to stop this."}, {"id": "questions_17_fault_tolerance_degradation_over_failure", "label": "Degradation over failure", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/questions/17-fault-tolerance.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Returning four of five sections beats a 500; failing the whole aggregation on one unhealthy dependency multiplies the platform's failure rate by four rather than hiding it."}, {"id": "questions_17_fault_tolerance_chaos_endpoints", "label": "Chaos endpoints (/chaos/fail)", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/questions/17-fault-tolerance.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Resilience code is the least-tested code in most systems because its conditions are hard to reproduce, so triggering them was made a one-line curl. Measured: first call 1038ms, subsequent calls ~15ms with BrokenCircuitException, KPI unaffected throughout."}], "edges": [{"source": "questions_17_fault_tolerance_four_layers", "target": "questions_17_fault_tolerance_question", "relation": "references", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1.0, "source_file": "docs/questions/17-fault-tolerance.md", "source_location": null, "weight": 1.0}, {"source": "questions_17_fault_tolerance_bulkhead", "target": "questions_17_fault_tolerance_four_layers", "relation": "rationale_for", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1.0, "source_file": "docs/questions/17-fault-tolerance.md", "source_location": null, "weight": 1.0}, {"source": "questions_17_fault_tolerance_cascading_failure", "target": "questions_17_fault_tolerance_four_layers", "relation": "rationale_for", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1.0, "source_file": "docs/questions/17-fault-tolerance.md", "source_location": null, "weight": 1.0}, {"source": "questions_17_fault_tolerance_degradation_over_failure", "target": "questions_17_fault_tolerance_four_layers", "relation": "rationale_for", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1.0, "source_file": "docs/questions/17-fault-tolerance.md", "source_location": null, "weight": 1.0}, {"sou